+## 3. The transaction and its ownership states
+
+A group is one `(ggml_context *, ggml_backend_buffer_type_t)` pair — one entry of `ml.ctx_map`.
+
+| state | set by | what the group owns | how it is discharged |
+|---|---|---|---|
+| censused | `add()` | nothing; only a size list | `free()` releases nothing for it |
+| reserved | `commit()` | entries `[plan_base, plan_base + n_ranges)` of its buffer type's plan | either consumed, or released by `free()` |
+| consumed | `alloc()` returning non-NULL | nothing; the bytes now belong to the caller's buffers | the caller freeing those buffers |
+
+Each state is a field assignment made **after** the operation it records succeeded, so no partial
+transition exists. Concretely:
+
+- **infeasible later group ⇒ zero driver allocations anywhere.** `commit()` opens every buffer
+  type's plan before any group allocates. On the first `INFEASIBLE` (or `INDETERMINATE` with more
+  than one entry) it calls `ggml_backend_alloc_plan_release_plans()` and returns false; the loader
+  throws. Proven by `test_loader_transaction_refuses_before_any_allocation` — the *first* buffer
+  type is the feasible one, and its `alloc_calls` is 0.
+- **exactly-once discharge.** `release_plans()` NULLs each plan pointer as it frees it, so the
+  commit-failure path and the destructor cannot free the same plan twice. Within a plan, an entry is
+  either adopted by `plan_alloc_buffer` (ledger untouched, ownership moves into the buffer) or
+  released by `plan_free`, never both — the Vulkan side already had this invariant; the test seam
+  now asserts it (`GGML_ASSERT(!plan->consumed[i])`, `GGML_ASSERT(plan->reserved == 0)`).
+- **mid-load failure.** If `alloc()` fails partway through a group, `alloc_tensor_range()` frees the
+  buffers that group already built — releasing the entries it had adopted — and the group stays
+  `reserved`, so `free()` returns everything it never consumed. Net reservation after the failure is
+  zero, asserted in `test_loader_transaction_mid_load_failure_rolls_back`.
+- **exception safety.** `load_tensors()` throws on several paths between `commit()` and the last
+  `alloc()`. The transaction is held in a `std::unique_ptr` with a deleter calling
+  `ggml_backend_alloc_plan_free`, so an escaping exception still releases every unconsumed
+  reservation. Without that, the device ledger would stay charged for bytes nothing holds and would
+  refuse the *next* load — strictly worse than the inherited behaviour.
+- **device / type / heap identity is never interpreted here.** The transaction groups by buffer-type
+  pointer only. Memory type index and heap index stay inside the Vulkan planner; the per-device
+  ledger is a member of the device (B7a-R2 finding 3) and is unchanged.
+- **no over-admission across concurrent loads.** Two transactions against the same buffer type
+  serialise inside `vk_heap_ledger::reserve_plan()` under its own lock, and an open plan's
+  reservations are visible to the next `plan_begin`. No loader-level lock was added, deliberately —
+  see §7.
+- **mixed backends.** A buffer type that answers `UNSUPPORTED` gets `plan == NULL` and its groups
+  allocate through the identical unplanned code path. CPU and every non-planning backend in a mixed
+  load are semantically unchanged; `plan_begin` is never called on them.
+- **census stability is checked, not assumed.** `alloc()` re-runs the census for its group and
+  refuses if the range count, the byte total, or any range boundary differs from what was reserved.
+
+## 4. Preserved behaviour
+
+- `ggml_backend_alloc_ctx_tensors_from_buft()` / `_size()` / `ggml_backend_alloc_ctx_tensors()`:
